  data:  # 55cd
    kind: invariant  # 55cd
    title: "Output projection prunes graph but leaves feature references dangling"  # 55cd
    status: violated  # 55cd
    priority: 1  # 55cd
    parent:  # 55cd
    tags:  # 55cd
    - dogfood_corpus_20260528  # 55cd
    - dogfood_integrated  # 55cd
    - dogfood_severity_significant  # 55cd
    isbefore: []  # 55cd
    duplicate_of: []  # 55cd
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 55cd
    pr_ref:  # 55cd
    description: "The `compact` subcommand produces a budget-limited projection of the behavior map, selecting a subset of nodes and edges via centrality heuristics. The top-level `nodes` and `edges` in the compact output are internally consistent — every edge endpoint appears in the node set. However, the `features` array (named slices of the graph for specific entry points) carries the original full-graph `node_ids` and `edge_ids` references unchanged, even when the referenced nodes and edges have been pruned. At small budget sizes, the great majority of feature references point at content that is not present. The pattern persists at larger budgets — at 10× the default budget, the same fraction of feature references still dangle — indicating the issue is structural rather than size-dependent.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) At default budget (`--max-symbols 100`), most feature references dangle.** Compact emits 100 nodes / 350 edges with all 16 features preserved unchanged. Reference check: `feature[0]` has `edge_ids=[4 IDs]` but only 1 of those edge IDs exists in the compact's 350 edges; `node_ids=[5 IDs]` but only 2 exist in the 100 nodes. `feature[7]` has `edge_ids=[19 IDs]` with only 2 present and `node_ids=[17 IDs]` with only 4 present. Features 8-14 each have `edge_ids=[]` and `node_ids=[1 ID]` with 0 of those node IDs present. Compact pass copies features wholesale from the source behavior map without filtering or rewriting referenced IDs.\n\n**(2) The dangling-rate is structural, not size-dependent.** Retested with `--max-symbols 1000` (10× the default): 1000 nodes / 8254 edges. Total: 10/70 feature `edge_ids` present, 20/71 feature `node_ids` present. Per-feature: `f0=1/4 edges, 2/5 nodes`; `f1=0/7, 1/6`; `f2=1/7, 2/6`; `f3=0/7, 1/6`; `f4=3/9, 4/8`. Dangling rate is ~85% even with 10× more symbols in the compact pool. Conclusion: increasing `--max-symbols` does NOT shrink the dangling-references count proportionally. The compact selection criteria (centrality coverage) don't co-vary with feature membership; feature-bearing node IDs are not being preferentially selected.\n\n**(3) Top-level `edges` / `nodes` self-consistency is 100% (350/350 endpoints present at default; 8254/8254 at 1k); only `features.edge_ids` / `features.node_ids` have dangling refs.** The compact's own primary graph is internally consistent; the dangling-references pattern is isolated to the features projection.\n\n### Invariant\n\nFeatures should be re-projected during compact construction — either dropping entries whose membership has been pruned below a threshold, expanding the node selection to preserve feature membership, or rewriting feature references to point only at retained nodes and edges. The features array's claim to describe slices of the compact's own graph requires that its referenced IDs exist in the compact output.\n\n### Scope\n\nEvery feature in every compact output. Dangling rate is consistently high (~85% even at 10× default budget) — the great majority of references dangle at typical budgets, and the pattern is structural.\n\n### Exposure\n\n`behavior_map.features[]` in `compact` output, specifically `features[].edge_ids` and `features[].node_ids` arrays.\n\n### Consequence\n\nConsumers using compact output as a self-contained subset of the behavior map find that feature references cannot be followed. Compact output's claim to be a coherent reduction is not honored on the features section — downstream tooling that walks features by ID hits missing data on ~85% of references. The dangling pattern means compact is unusable as a self-contained corpus for any feature-walking analysis.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Re-project `features[].edge_ids` and `features[].node_ids` during compact construction: filter each feature's referenced IDs to the post-compaction node/edge set.\n- Alternatively, expand the compact's node selection to preserve feature membership — when a feature is included, include all the nodes and edges its IDs reference (treat features as additional inclusion seeds alongside centrality).\n- For features whose membership has been pruned below a threshold (e.g., <2 retained references), drop the feature entry entirely rather than emitting a near-empty reference list.\n- Add a property test that asserts every `features[].edge_ids` ID and every `features[].node_ids` ID is present in the compact's own `edges` / `nodes` arrays.\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-zavin — which is deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 55cd
    fields:  # 55cd
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-857994"  # 55cd
      statement: "Features should be re-projected during compact construction — either dropping entries whose membership has been pruned below a threshold, expanding the node selection to preserve feature membership, or rewriting feature references to point only at retained nodes and edges."  # 55cd
      root_cause: "A subcommand that produces a compact projection of the behavior map selects a budget-limited set of nodes and edges via centrality heuristics. The top-level nodes and edges in the compact output are internally consistent — every edge endpoint appears in the node set. However, the features array (named slices of the graph for specific entry points) carries the original full-graph node-id and edge-id references unchanged, even when the referenced nodes and edges have been pruned from the compact output. At small budget sizes, the great majority of feature references point at content that is not present. The pattern persists at larger budgets — at ten times the default budget, the same fraction of feature references still dangle, indicating the issue is structural (centrality-based selection does not co-vary with feature membership) rather than size-dependent."  # 55cd
- op: discuss  # b1c3
  at: '2026-05-30T09:06:43Z'  # b1c3
  by: agent  # b1c3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b1c3
  clock: 2  # b1c3
  nonce: b1c3  # b1c3
  message: "Cross-ref backfill (2026-05-30, deeper-issue investigation): Confirmed mechanism. compact.py:1054 (connectivity branch) and compact.py:1073 (coverage branch) both do compact_map = dict(behavior_map), then rebuild nodes/edges/entrypoints but never touch features. The shallow copy preserves the original features wholesale, including their node_ids/edge_ids references to content that the centrality selection has pruned. Three structural fix-directions: (i) filter features[].node_ids and features[].edge_ids against included_ids, (ii) drop features whose surviving membership falls below a threshold, (iii) seed feature membership into the selection set before pruning. Twin to INV-tarol — both are \"feature scope not re-derived from emitted graph.\" Adjacent closed item WI-posuf addressed a different compact-output ID-resolution issue (hash-format endpoints) on the same compact-output surface."  # b1c3
- op: discuss  # 67b2
  at: '2026-06-02T22:52:14Z'  # 67b2
  by: agent  # 67b2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 67b2
  clock: 3  # 67b2
  nonce: 67b2  # 67b2
  message: "Pass 39 (2026-06-02, dogfooding) — F39.D1 substantive quantification at compact subcommand scope:\n\nINV-titid title 'Output projection prunes graph but leaves feature references dangling' is QUANTIFIED and SCOPED to compact subcommand at massive rates. Generated compact substrates against /tmp/hg-dogfood-s21/bm.json at default (connectivity-aware) and --no-connectivity with coverage 0.1/0.5/0.8/0.99:\n\n| Mode | nodes | feats | entry_nodes unresolved | node_ids unresolved | edge_ids unresolved | EP.symbol_id unresolved |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| default | 100 | 16 | 8/16 (50%) | 36/71 (50.7%) | 32/70 (45.7%) | 0/33 |\n| noconn cov=0.1 | 57 | 16 | 8/16 (50%) | 58/71 (81.7%) | 66/70 (94.3%) | 0/33 |\n| noconn cov=0.5+ | 100 | 16 | 8/16 (50%) | 58/71 (81.7%) | 66/70 (94.3%) | 0/33 |\n\nPattern:\n- entrypoint.symbol_id 100% resolved (compact code at compact.py:1065 + 1089 filters entrypoints to those whose symbol_id exists in included_ids)\n- Feature references UNFILTERED — features array passed through unchanged regardless of which nodes were dropped. Result: 50% of feature.entry_nodes, 50-82% of feature.node_ids, 46-94% of feature.edge_ids reference symbols/edges absent from the compact substrate.\n\nFix-path: parallel feature filter at compact.py:1094 (where compact_map is constructed). Either drop features whose entry_nodes are all absent, OR rewrite feature.node_ids / edge_ids to drop out-of-substrate references and flag the feature as truncated. Cousin pattern to F37.B4 (missing entrypoints_truncated marker on compact substrate) — both point at compact subcommand lacking a 'this feature/EP was projected away' signal. Cosmically the spec.md feature record doesn't have a 'truncated' field at all (per pass-37 F37.B4 cumulative).\n\nSubstrate evidence: compact at default produces 16 features but only 8 of their entry_nodes survive in the 100-node compact set. Consumer reading compact.json sees 16 features each claiming an entry_node ID; trying to render any of the 8 unresolved features would fail or display empty/dangling. INV-titid's 'leaves feature references dangling' is empirically valid at 50%+ on top-level invariant; 80-94% on slice-payload fields under noconn."  # 67b2
- op: discuss  # 1af4
  at: '2026-06-03T00:10:06Z'  # 1af4
  by: agent  # 1af4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1af4
  clock: 4  # 1af4
  nonce: 1af4  # 1af4
  message: "Pass-40 F40.A1+A2+A3 batch — compact substrate self-description gaps (3 sibling findings).\n\nF40.A1 (metrics-echo): /tmp/p40_compact_default.json contains 100 nodes + 370 edges + 33 EPs in its arrays, but the metrics block reports pre-compact totals: metrics.total_nodes=34253, metrics.total_edges=109915, metrics.total_files=915. metrics.by_supply_chain_tier likewise reports first_party.nodes=32531 / edges=109892 — i.e. the source numbers. A consumer reading the compact file's metrics block gets the SOURCE size, not the VIEW size. Distinct from F39.D1 (dangling feature refs) — this is top-level summary statistics disagreeing with their own document body.\n\nF40.A2 (analysis_incomplete flag): The compact substrate sets analysis_incomplete=false while nodes_summary.omitted.count=34153 (99.71% truncated) and 71/104 EPs (68%) dropped. The flag tracks analyzer failures only, not view-truncation. For compact substrates this field is structurally misleading: a downstream consumer checking analysis_incomplete to decide whether to trust metrics gets False on a dramatically incomplete view. Fix path candidates: (a) flip analysis_incomplete=True when view==compact, or (b) leave it and add view_is_truncated peer field.\n\nF40.A3 (EP-level truncation marker absent): nodes_summary has rich structured truncation — included.{count,centrality_sum,coverage} + omitted.{count:34153, centrality_sum:115.29, max_centrality:1.40, kinds (26 entries: method:18286/function:7045/class:4440/external_symbol:1618/variable:1608/file:883/call_site:130/dependency:82/export:20/interface:7), tiers, top_paths:5, top_words:10}. No parallel entrypoints_summary, features_summary, or usage_contexts_summary. The 71 dropped EPs are recoverable only by comparison with the source bm.json — the compact file alone cannot tell you 71 EPs were dropped. Strict refinement of F37.B4: marker missing for EPs specifically, not generally absent. Symmetry fix: add entrypoints_summary.{included,omitted.{count,kinds,packages}}.\n\nCombined: 3 sibling self-description gaps at the compact writer (cli.py compact subcommand). One fix point — propagate VIEW-state into top-level summary fields when truncating."  # 1af4
